    "reply": "The only gene record returned for this query is NCBIGene:672 [1]. Its name is BRCA1 DNA repair associated [1]. The rest of the returned records are ClinVar sequence variants on the NM_007294.4 transcript [2].\n\n## Overlapping gene\n\nThe gene record that bears on this interval is NCBIGene:672 [1]. The retrieved records do not include any dbVar structural-variant entry or copy-number record, so there is no direct CNV evidence here.\n\n## ClinVar entries\n\nThe ClinVar entries are single-nucleotide missense variants rather than copy-number changes. One example is NM_007294.4(BRCA1):c.353T>C (p.Leu118Pro) [4]. Another is NM_007294.4(BRCA1):c.4709T>C (p.Leu1570Pro) [2]. A further entry is NM_007294.4(BRCA1):c.1226T>G (p.Val409Gly) [5]. Another is NM_007294.4(BRCA1):c.3664G>A (p.Glu1222Lys) [3]. One more entry is NM_007294.4(BRCA1):c.4652C>T (p.Ser1551Phe) [15]. One record has only an identifier, ClinVar:1010631 [30].\n\n## Limits for ACMG scoring\n\nNone of these records states a clinical significance, so they cannot be used as CNV pathogenicity evidence without a separate lookup. They do show that ClinVar holds many sequence variants in the gene [1]. For the CNV itself, the next step would be a live dbVar and ClinVar coordinate-overlap query.",
